# Overview

There are two coordinate spaces commonly used. The first is "mosaic space" (also called section space) which is the position on a specific section's 2D image. The second is "Volume Space" which is the position after a section has been transformed into a volume. In a drawing context there may also be a screen coordinate space.

# Mosaic vs volume

- **Mosaic / section space** — pixels on one section's assembled image (a `.mosaic` or a tileset grid).
- **Volume space** — that section after a slice-to-slice (stos) warp into the volume. When the volume transform is `None` or missing, mosaic == volume (identity).
- **Screen space** — pixels in the viewer. Camera / downsample convert volume ↔ screen.

`IVolumeToSectionTransform.SectionToVolume` is mosaic → volume. `VolumeToSection` is volume → mosaic. The copy-pasted comments that said both methods map "volume to section" were wrong.

On a stos `ITransform`, `Transform` is section → volume (mapped → control). `ControlBounds` is volume; `MappedBounds` is mosaic.

# Tileset vs pyramid

- **Tileset** — warp is already in the tile images. `MappingManager` cache key uses the **channel name**. `Initialized` is immediately true. `ControlBounds` is the mosaic grid even after `TileGridToVolumeMapping` applies a stos for annotations.
- **Pyramid** — raw tiles plus a mosaic stos in `Section.WarpedTo`. Cache key uses the **section transform name**; `CurrentPyramid` is then set to the channel. `Initialized` stays false until `Initialize` / Warp finishes. `SectionSceneRenderer` must start that work or `DrawTiles` returns every frame.

# Which type to use

- **Draw tiles** — `MappingManager.GetMapping` (picks tileset vs pyramid and optional volume stos).
- **Annotation hit-test / region queries** — `VolumeTransformProvider` / `IVolumeToSectionTransform` (stos only, no tiles).
- **Region queries** — map the visible volume rect with `ApproximateVisibleMosaicBounds`. Null means skip the request, not "empty region".
